when most people think about the Cold  War they think about two ideological  factions vying for global supremacy what  isn't often talked about is the  infighting which occurred in both camps  the most important of these conflicts  was the sino-soviet split which sold the  People's Republic of China denounced the  USSR and go its own way so what caused  the split and what long-term effects did  it have prior to the Second World War  China was embroiled in a civil war which  saw the Communists fighting against the  Chinese Republic this conflict was  halted when the Japanese invaded and  both sides allied against the foreign  threat which was due in part to a great  deal of pressure from a certain Joseph  Stalin the Japanese lost and by the time  of their surrender China looked like  this with the USSR occupying Manchuria  which had promptly handed over to the  Communists and the leader Mao Zedong the  civil war resumed and in the end the  Communists won given the USSR a new  powerful ally both sides signed a  friendship treaty in 1950 and so things  were looking up for both of them also  unlike the Communist States in Eastern  Europe China was an independent nation a  Moscow couldn't appoint or dismiss its  leaders nor control its internal policy  since it was independent it's led to the  Chinese leadership applying the  communist model in a fundamentally  different way to the USSR Stalinism and  Maoism were rapidly diverging and this  difference became even more pronounced  when Stalin died and was succeeded by  Nikita Khrushchev the divisions between  the two communist States span both  internal and external policies in terms  of internal policy the USSR disagreed  with Mao's Great Leap Forward in its  implementation mouthes approached a  change for the Chinese government  hastily reformed agriculture and demand  that it output unreasonable amounts  these policies exacerbated a major  drought which hit China between 1959 and  1961 which saw the deaths of at least 15  million people to make matters worse Mao  also opted to continue with the policy  of exporting grain because he wanted  China to look like it was a wealthy  well-fed country furthermore Mao  disliked the reforms undertaken by  Nikita Khrushchev which he felt were  fundamentally on communist Mao also saw  Khrushchev's use of the Red Army to put  down the 1956 Hungarian Revolution as de  legitimizing communism internationally  which brings us on to conflicts of a  foreign policy  Khrushchev had taken a different  approach to the USSR's relationship with  the West and had pursued a policy of  peaceful coexistence when a series of  foreign policy clashes between the USSR  and the USA ended with Khrushchev  backing down Mao decided that communism  needed a new leader one who would  confront the West on all fronts at every  opportunity while  these may sound like issues which could  have been papered over for Mao and  Khrushchev ideological purity was a  matter of life or death for communism in  the end these disagreements led to the  USSR formally breaking off diplomatic  relations with the PRC in late 1961 in  mid 1962 Mao opted to break off  relations with the USSR and hereafter  the Communist world was fractured and  would remain so during the Cold War  China never managed to take leadership  of the Communist world but it did have  some communist states which opted to  follow the principles of Maoism and was  the division of the Communist states of  the world was overwhelmingly in favor of  the Soviet Union EDA presented with new  difficulties the 1960s saw China embrace  a much more aggressive foreign policy  which saw it develop its own nuclear  weapons and also fight border conflicts  with Taiwan India and also the USSR  although in the end Mouse which from an  ideological foreign policy to a  pragmatic one which saw him restore and  even improve relations with the US in  the West the People's Republic of China  Hereafter pursued its own goals and  given that it outlasted the USSR the  sino-soviet split didn't seem to  negatively affect it too much I hope you  enjoyed this episode and thank you for  watching with extra thanks to my patreon  supporters which you can see on screen  and with an extra special thanks to  James Bissonnette David archaeologist  Ozark a flash Party Boyko Rob Waterhouse  Chris wicker Michael Reynolds Gustav  Swan David Silverman spinning three  plates  Maggie packs Kowski Christian check'  Anthony Beckett Ike Skye Chappell and  Winston K word  you
 

